<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    GH-1537: "Compiler use of XMM registers for memset badly recognized"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/1537

    Source:   char buf[64];  void f(void) { memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf)); }
    GCC -O2 inlines the memset as one pxor xmm0,xmm0 plus four 128-bit
    movaps stores (RIP-relative to the global).  The decompiler decomposes
    the wide SIMD zero-stores into 64 individual element writes
    (buf[0]='\0'; ... buf[0x3f]='\0';) and never reconstructs the memset.

    Bytes compiled from the issue's exact source on this machine (gcc -O2),
    RIP displacements resolved for f@0x100000, buf@0x101000.

    Stage model: S5 const-sequence - the SAME sub-stage decision as GH-9230
    (constant-fill run recovery), exercised here on a GLOBAL destination and
    RIP-relative SIMD stores rather than GH-9230's stack buffer.  No new
    code: the fix is pure ROUTING through the existing P0 assertion
    `option memsetrecover on` (kuna_memsetsequence, GH-9230) - direct
    evidence that an exposed sub-stage generalizes across issues.

    Pass 1 (default): assertion #1 reproduces the bug (element stores).
    Pass 2 (memsetrecover on): assertion #2 proves the single memset.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
f30f1efa660fefc00f2905f10f00000f
2905fa0f00000f2905031000000f2905
0c100000c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void f(void);</com>
  <com>map address r0x101000 char buf[64]</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option memsetrecover on</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GH1537 #1: SIMD zero-fill decomposed into element stores (bug reproduced)" min="1" max="1">buf\[0x3f\] = '\\0';</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH1537 #2: memsetrecover on reconstructs the single memset (routing fix via the GH-9230 sub-stage)" min="1" max="1">builtin_memset\(buf,0,0x40\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
